<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso II of Asturias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 759–<a href="/years/842">842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Alfonso the Chaste</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso II, called the Chaste, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> from 791 to 842. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> his capital, modelled his court and church on the Visigothic kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, resisted repeated Umayyad raids and kept contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. During his reign the supposed tomb of Saint James was discovered at Compostela, and he built the first church there, beginning a great pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-asturias.jpg" alt="Alfonso II of Asturias" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pelagius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfonsobig.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfonso was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> around 759 or 760, the son of King Fruela I and Munia, a Basque woman whom Fruela had captured on a campaign and brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>. After his father's death he was placed under the guardianship of his aunt Adosinda, though one tradition has him sent to the monastery of San Xulián de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>.</p><p>He was governor of the palace under Adosinda's husband Silo. When Silo died, Adosinda's allies elected Alfonso king, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> raised his uncle Mauregatus to the throne, and Alfonso fled to Álava, where his maternal relatives sheltered him. Mauregatus was followed by Alfonso's cousin Bermudo I, who abdicated after defeat at the Battle of the Burbia River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Alfonso was elected king on 14 September 791. He moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravia">Pravia</a>, where Silo had placed it, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, the city his father had founded and where he was born, and there built churches and a palace, among them San Tirso, where he is buried, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-julian-de-los-prados">San Julián de los Prados</a>. Hisham I began campaigns on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and in the north-west, and in 794 a raid led by Abd al-Karim dealt a major blow to Alfonso on the eastern edge of his kingdom, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, after which the raiders pillaged deeper into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>.</p><p>Alfonso sent embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and Aix-la-Chapelle in 796, 797 and 798. The Royal Frankish Annals acknowledge him as a king and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> as a kingdom for the first time. Troops from Asturias captured and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 798.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tradition says that in 814 the body of Saint James was found at Compostela and that Alfonso was the first pilgrim to the shrine at Libredón. In 825 he defeated Saracen forces at Narón, near Ferrol, and at Anceo, and these victories began the resettlement of parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, confirmed by charters. He died in 842 after a reign of 51 years, and the Crónica Sebastianense praises his government as chaste, sober, immaculate, pious and glorious.</p><p>After his death Nepotian tried to take the crown in place of Ramiro I. Later poets invented a story of a secret marriage between Alfonso's sister Ximena and Sancho, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saldana">Saldaña</a>, and of the exploits of their son Bernardo del Carpio.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfonso II of Asturias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Fruela I founded Oviedo around 761, and <b>Alfonso II</b> later made it the permanent royal seat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-5.85&amp;year=812" aria-label="Oviedo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4409 625 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alfonso II of Asturias"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4859 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4859 895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-5.85&amp;year=812">What was here in 812 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
